-- title:
--   Integral quotient datum for a Galois-stable subgroup of order p
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $\mathbb{Z}$ with $\Delta(W)\neq 0$, let $p$ be a prime with $p\neq 2$, and write $W_{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}}$ for the base change of $W$ along $\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Q}$ and then to $\mathrm{AlgebraicClosure}\,\mathbb{Q}$, whose affine point group is $(W_{\bar{\mathbb{Q}}})(\bar{\mathbb{Q}})$. Let $L$ be an additive subgroup of this point group whose number of elements is $p$ and which is stable under the action of every $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ (the action being coordinatewise application of $\sigma$ to affine points, fixing the point at infinity). The assertion is that there exist a Weierstrass curve $W'$ over $\mathbb{Z}$ and an additive group homomorphism $\varphi$ from the $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $W$ to the $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $W'$ such that: $\Delta(W')\neq 0$; $\ker\varphi=L$ exactly; $\varphi(\sigma\cdot x)=\sigma\cdot\varphi(x)$ for all $\sigma$ and all $x$; and, for every prime $q$ with $q\neq p$, $q\mid\Delta(W)$ and $q\nmid c_4(W)$, one has $q\mid\Delta(W')$, $q\nmid c_4(W')$, and for every valuation subring $A$ of $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ in which $q$ is a non-unit and every point $y$ of $W(\bar{\mathbb{Q}})$, the point $\varphi(y)$ satisfies [`WeierstrassCurve.InZeroComponentAt`](def/EllipticCurve_ZeroComponentAt.html#L13) for $W'$ at $A$ if and only if $y-k$ satisfies it for $W$ at $A$ for some $k\in L$. Here `InZeroComponentAt` for a curve and a place $A$ holds of a point $P$ when either $P=0$, or $P$ is affine with $x$-coordinate outside $A$, or $P$ is affine with both coordinates in $A$ and their images in the residue field of $A$ a nonsingular point of the reduced Weierstrass curve. No surjectivity of $\varphi$ is claimed, and nothing is claimed at $q=p$ or at primes where $W$ has good or additive reduction: the local information is supplied prime by prime, only at the multiplicative primes away from $p$.
--
--   Classically $W'$ is an integral model of the quotient curve $W/L$ and $\varphi$ the quotient isogeny, constructed by Vélu's formulas; the transport statement for the zero component is the Tate-curve computation at a multiplicative prime $q\neq p$, where $L$ is either $\mu_p$ inside the zero component or is generated by a $p$-th root of the Tate parameter, and in both cases $\varphi^{-1}(W'^{0}_A)=W^{0}_A+L$. The formal statement is shaped to be read prime by prime: it asserts multiplicative type of $W'$ and the transport law only at those primes $q\neq p$ at which $W$ is already of multiplicative type in the explicit sense $q\mid\Delta$, $q\nmid c_4$, it makes no minimality or semistability claim at $p$, and it does not assert that $\varphi$ is onto. It is used in the argument of Mazur's Step 3 style, for the rung [`FreyPackage.frey_no_cofixed_large`](thm.html#FreyPackage.frey_no_cofixed_large) excluding a Galois-stable cofixed line for the Frey curve when $P.p\geq 17$.
-- source:
